course: geography
lesson: 38
title: Today's world: the UN, the EU and the Commonwealth
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 countries deal with each other only through alliances, marriages and wars, but today almost every country also sits in big clubs where they talk, vote and make rules together.
explain: The biggest is the United Nations, founded in 1945 after the Second World War, so that countries would argue at a table instead of on a battlefield; it has 193 members and its headquarters is in New York.
explain: Think of a school council: everyone has a vote, but in the UN Security Council five permanent members can each block a decision on their own, which is a bit like five pupils who can veto any plan.
explain: Old rivals from the game, such as France, Spain, Portugal and Italy, now work together in the European Union, sharing one market and many laws, and most of them even share one currency, the euro.
explain: Other clubs join countries by history or by place: the Commonwealth links many lands once in the British Empire, NATO is a promise to defend each other, and the African Union links the whole of Africa.
fact: The United Nations was founded in 1945 after the Second World War to keep peace and help countries work together; it has 193 member states and its headquarters is in New York.
fact: The UN Security Council has five permanent members, the USA, Russia, China, the UK and France, each of whom can veto a decision.
fact: Many countries argue that the Security Council is out of date because no country from Africa or Latin America has a permanent seat.
fact: The UN has agencies for special tasks, such as UNICEF for children, the World Health Organization for health and the World Food Programme for hunger.
fact: The World Health Organization helped to wipe out smallpox, whose last natural case was in 1977.
fact: The European Union is a group of 27 European countries that share a single market, many common laws and, for 21 of them since Bulgaria joined in 2026, the euro.
fact: The United Kingdom left the European Union in 2020 after a referendum, a vote of all the people, in 2016; this is called Brexit.
fact: The Commonwealth of Nations is a voluntary group of 56 countries, most of which were once part of the British Empire, though some members, such as Rwanda and Mozambique, never were.
fact: The British monarch is also head of state of 14 other Commonwealth countries, such as Australia, Canada, New Zealand and Jamaica, which are called Commonwealth realms, and Barbados became a republic in 2021.
fact: NATO is a military alliance of 32 countries in Europe and North America whose members promise to defend each other if attacked; Finland joined in 2023 and Sweden in 2024.
fact: The African Union links all 55 African countries to work together on peace, trade and development, and its headquarters is in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia.
fact: The International Court of Justice in The Hague settles legal disputes between countries, and The Hague also hosts the International Criminal Court, which can try people for war crimes.
exercise: When was the United Nations founded? | 1945 | 1918 | 1991 | 1337
exercise: How many member states does the UN have? | 193 | 27 | 56 | 500
exercise: Where is the UN's headquarters? | New York | Brussels | Geneva, Switzerland | London, England
exercise: Which countries are the five permanent members of the Security Council? | The USA, Russia, China, the UK and France | The USA, Germany, Japan, India and Brazil | The UK, France, Spain, Italy and Portugal | China, India, Brazil, Nigeria and Egypt
exercise: What can a permanent member of the Security Council do that others cannot? | Block a decision with a veto | Move the UN headquarters | Vote twice on every question | Command other armies
exercise: Why do many countries say the Security Council is out of date? | No African or Latin American country has a permanent seat | It meets only once every hundred years, so it never decides anything | It has no members at all | It is run by the European Union
exercise: Which UN agency works for children? | UNICEF | The NATO alliance | The World Food Programme | The African Union
exercise: Which disease was wiped out with the help of the WHO? | Smallpox | The flu virus | Measles everywhere | The common cold
exercise: How many countries are in the European Union? | 27 | 193 | 56 | 32
exercise: How many EU countries use the euro? | 21 | 27 | 12 | All of Europe
exercise: When did the United Kingdom leave the European Union? | 2020 | 2016 | 1945 | 2007
exercise: What is the Commonwealth of Nations? | A voluntary group of 56 countries, mostly once in the British Empire | An army of 56 countries that has to defend Britain whenever it is attacked | A single country ruled from London | A group of all European countries
exercise: Is the King head of state of Australia? | Yes, Australia is one of the Commonwealth realms | No, Australia has no head of state | No, Australia's head of state is the UN Secretary-General | Yes, but only of Sydney
exercise: Which country became a republic in 2021? | Barbados | Jamaica | Canada | Belize
exercise: What do NATO members promise each other? | To defend each other if attacked | To use the same money, the euro | To speak the same language | To have no borders between them
exercise: Which country joined NATO in 2024? | Sweden | Finland | Ukraine | Ireland
exercise: Where is the African Union's headquarters? | Addis Ababa | Cairo, Egypt | Lagos, Nigeria | Nairobi, Kenya
exercise: Where is the International Court of Justice? | The Hague | New York City | Paris, France | Geneva, Switzerland
test: my mate reckons Brexit happened in 2016. not quite, is it | The vote was in 2016, but the UK left in 2020 | Yes, the UK left the EU on the very day of the vote | No, Brexit happened in 1945 | No, the UK is still in the EU
test: If France vetoes a Security Council decision that every other member supports, what happens? | The decision is blocked | It passes anyway | France is thrown out of the UN | The vote is moved to the EU
test: A friend says every Commonwealth country was once a British colony. What is wrong? | Some, like Rwanda and Mozambique, never were | Nothing, every member was a colony | The Commonwealth has no members at all, only a flag | Only France is in it
test: In EU5, France and Spain fight war after war. What are they now? | Partners in the European Union | Still at war every year | One single country | Both British colonies
test: You travel from Italy to Germany with euros in your pocket. Why don't you need to change money? | Both use the euro | Germany has no money of its own | All 27 EU countries use the same notes | Italy uses German money
test: Which group links all 55 countries of Africa? | The African Union | The Commonwealth | NATO | The European Union
test: oi, is the UN some kind of world government that bosses everyone? | No, it's a club of countries that talk and work together | Yes, it rules every country directly | Yes, its army controls all borders | No, it only runs New York
test: A general is accused of war crimes. Which court in The Hague can try him? | The International Criminal Court | The International Court of Justice | The UN Security Council | The European Parliament
test: If Finland had not joined NATO, how many members would NATO have? | 31 | 33 | 27 | 56
test: Jamaica's head of state is the British monarch. What does this make Jamaica? | A Commonwealth realm | A member of the EU | A part of the UK | A NATO member
test: Your cousin says the UN only argues and never does anything useful. Which fact answers him? | The WHO helped wipe out smallpox | The UN built the Great Wall | The UN ended the Hundred Years' War | The UN drew the Tordesillas line
exam: State the year of foundation of the United Nations and the number of its present member states. | 1945, with one hundred and ninety-three member states | 1918, with fifty-one member states | 1991, with twenty-seven member states | 1945, with fifty-six member states
exam: Identify the prerogative peculiar to the permanent members of the Security Council of the United Nations. | Each may veto a decision of the Council | Each may expel any other member state | Each may relocate the headquarters | Each may cast a double vote in the General Assembly
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Commonwealth of Nations? | A voluntary association of fifty-six states, the majority formerly within the British Empire | A military alliance obliged to mutual defence | A single sovereign state governed from London | A customs union of twenty-seven European states
exam: Ascertain the date upon which the United Kingdom withdrew from the European Union, the referendum having been held in 2016. | 2020 | 2016 | 1973 | 2007
